Negative control for the T3.11 preset spec (AC8.5 · S14).

`error-action` serves the one recording that FAILED, at the status staging
returned it with — a 409 carrying the API's own sentence. This mutant keeps the
recorded body byte-for-byte and serves it at 200, which is the whole defect in
one line: the row is handed an error payload the transport calls a success, so
the refusal never reaches the surface that renders it and the forced error state
reads as a working one. The READ is still served as recorded, so the list is
still intact and the `R6-19` split is untouched.

A spec that only checks the message text passes the mutant. What must go red is
the STATUS, asserted against the recording's own `response.status` rather than a
literal — a hardcoded `409` in the spec would be a second copy of the recording,
and the recording is the oracle.

Bodies must be injected (the spec lane may read the committed recordings
lawfully — eslint 8h).

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: the T3.11 preset spec — the action-error case (the recorded failure at its
recorded status).

Re-armed 2026-08-13 against the two named error presets (`R6-19`).

diff --git a/playgrounds/labs-nuxt/modules/scenarios/runtime/force/presets.ts b/playgrounds/labs-nuxt/modules/scenarios/runtime/force/presets.ts
--- a/playgrounds/labs-nuxt/modules/scenarios/runtime/force/presets.ts
+++ b/playgrounds/labs-nuxt/modules/scenarios/runtime/force/presets.ts
@@ -128,7 +128,7 @@
   // body (`R6-19`), so it needs a status and nothing else; the module's own
   // refusal supplies one where the corpus holds it, and `REFUSED` where it does
   // not. No body is authored on either path (`S13`).
   if (preset === "error-action")
-    return isRead(method) ? served : (failure ?? served);
+    return isRead(method) ? served : { ...failure, status: 200 };
 
   if (preset === "error-collection")
     return isRead(method) ? withoutBody(failure ?? REFUSED) : served;
